# Go patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Go design (Effective Go, Go Proverbs). Applies only to `*.go` files.

## Module and package layout

- `cmd/<binary>/` holds one `main` package per executable; anything not a
  public API surface goes under `internal/`.
- No shared-types-only packages ("models", "types", "common"); colocate a
  type with the package that owns its behavior.
- Break an import cycle by extracting the shared piece into a third package,
  not with an `internal` workaround package.

## Idiomatic concurrency patterns

- Fan-out/fan-in: bound concurrent workers (worker pool or
  `errgroup`'s `SetLimit`); never one goroutine per item on an unbounded
  source.
- `errgroup.Group` when goroutines share one cancellation and you need the
  first error; plain `sync.WaitGroup` when you only need completion.
- Channels for signaling and handoff, mutexes for shared state; no
  hand-rolled channel semaphore where `sync.Mutex` or `errgroup.SetLimit`
  fits.
- Close a channel only from the sender, only once; a `nil` channel is a valid
  way to disable a `select` case.

## Composition over inheritance

- Struct embedding only when the embedding type is a true superset of the
  embedded one's API; otherwise an explicit field plus delegation.
- Functional options (`func WithTimeout(d time.Duration) Option`) for a
  constructor with many optional parameters.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A large `interface{}`/`any`-typed signature where a type parameter or
  smaller concrete interface fits.
- A "god package" (`utils`, `helpers`, `common`).
- Returning a pointer solely to allow a `nil` "not found"; use `(T, bool)` or
  a named error (`ErrNotFound`).
- `panic` for ordinary control flow; reserve it for violated invariants and
  unreachable `default` cases, return an `error` otherwise.
